	(text_box "The Micromouse PCB is a two-layer robotic control board designed for an autonomous maze-solving robot. The board integrates power regulation, motor control, infrared wall detection, wheel encoder feedback, and microcontroller programming into a single platform. Its primary purpose is to support autonomous maze exploration and navigation by allowing an STM32 microcontroller to detect surrounding walls, monitor wheel movement, and independently control two DC motors.\n\nThe system uses an STM32 NUCLEO-F411RE development board as its main controller. Four infrared emitter and phototransistor pairs provide wall-detection signals, while two quadrature magnetic encoders provide wheel rotation feedback. An L293D dual H-bridge motor driver controls the direction of two brushed DC gearmotors.\n\nBoard Specifications\n\nPCB: Two-layer board, approximately 72 mm × 88.5 mm overall outline.\nMicrocontroller: STM32 NUCLEO-F411RE, based on the STM32F411RE ARM Cortex-M4 microcontroller.\nPower Input: External battery connected through a 2-pin connector (J1), with an onboard ON/OFF switch (SW1).\nVoltage Regulation: TPS62162DSG synchronous buck converter providing a nominal 3.3 V output, rated for up to 1 A, with a specified 3-17 V input operating range.\nMotor Driver: L293D dual H-bridge driver supporting independent control of two brushed DC motors, with a device output rating of up to 600 mA per channel.\nMotor Connections: Two 6-pin connectors (J3 and J4), each providing motor outputs, encoder power, ground, and two encoder feedback signals.\nInfrared Detection: Four INL-5AMIR15 IR emitters and four SFH313 phototransistors, with BS170 MOSFETs controlling the emitters.\nSensor Interfaces: Four GPIO-driven infrared transmitters and four sensor feedback channels connected to the microcontroller.\nEncoder Interfaces: Right motor encoder connected to PA0/PA1 and left motor encoder connected to PA8/PA9.\nMotor Control Interfaces: PB6, PB7, PB8, and PB9 connected to the L293D motor-control inputs.\nProgramming and Debugging: 4-pin SWD header (J7) providing SWCLK, SWDIO, RESET, and GND.\n\nThe board is intended to provide a compact hardware platform for implementing wall detection, encoder-based motion feedback, motor speed and direction control, and autonomous maze-navigation algorithms.\n\nDesign Status: The design is undergoing electrical and PCB revision review. The current schematic specifies the TPS62162 regulator, while the existing PCB layout still contains an LD1117V33 regulator. The L293D logic supply and enable connections also require correction or validation before fabrication."
